<td id="S5.T2.6.1.11.22" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFF2B2;padding:0.6pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T2.6.1.11.22.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFF2B2;">30.5</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5 Empirical Evaluations ‣ TaTToo: Tool-Grounded Thinking PRMfor Test-Time Scaling in Tabular Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports the Best-of-N performance of incorporating <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TaTToo</span>  on the DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-14B model across five tabular reasoning tasks.
Notably, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TaTToo</span>  consistently outperforms strong baselines such as GenPRM-32B and Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-72B despite using only 8B parameters.
On TB-DA, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TaTToo</span>  achieves the largest accruacy performance across each level of N, rising from 27.7% at N<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{=}4</annotation></semantics></math> to 34.3% at N<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{=}32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{=}32</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, while existing PRMs often suffer from performance bottlenecks beyond a certain response threshold (as observed in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Why Table Reasoning Requires Verifiers Beyond Current PRMs? ‣ TaTToo: Tool-Grounded Thinking PRMfor Test-Time Scaling in Tabular Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TaTToo</span>  continues to scale effectively, yielding consistent gains as the response group size increases.
For example, on TB-NR, Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-72B saturates after N<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{=}16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{=}16</annotation></semantics></math> (74.9% <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> 75.3%), whereas <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TaTToo</span>  continues to improve from 74.2% at N<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{=}8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{=}8</annotation></semantics></math> to 78.1% at N<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{=}32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{=}32</annotation></semantics></math>.
These results demonstrate that <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TaTToo</span>  provides stronger reward supervision on LRMs’ reasoning trajectories, therefore yielding better performance improvement compared with other step-verification baselines.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06217v1/analyses_TTS.png" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:466/141;" width="466" height="141" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Performance of <span id="S5.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TaTToo</span>  on two additional TTS strategies, Beam Search and Diverse Verifier Tree Search (DVTS). We report the average accuracy across all 5 tabular reasoning tasks.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generalizability on Other TTS Strategies.</span>
Beyond best-of-<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, we also evaluate <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TaTToo</span>  under two additional TTS strategies (Beam Search and DVTS) and compare with the strongest PRM baselines. Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.1 Main Results ‣ 5 Empirical Evaluations ‣ TaTToo: Tool-Grounded Thinking PRMfor Test-Time Scaling in Tabular Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> reports the average performance across the five tabular reasoning tasks. Under each TTS strategy, <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TaTToo</span>  consistently yields steady improvements as the number of responses N increases, whereas other baseline PRMs often plateau. For example, in beam search, <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TaTToo</span>  improves from 45.0% to 54.8%, while GenPRM saturates around 51% and Skywork-PRM remains below 46%. These results highlight the strong generalizability of <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TaTToo</span>  across diverse TTS strategies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>In-depth Analyses on <span id="S5.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TaTToo</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mastery of RL with Bootstrapping from SFT.</span>
To examine the respective roles of SFT and RL in <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TaTToo</span>’s dual-stage training paradigm, we compare against a variant <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TaTToo</span> (SFT only), which is trained solely on the first SFT stage.
As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2 In-depth Analyses on TaTToo ‣ 5 Empirical Evaluations ‣ TaTToo: Tool-Grounded Thinking PRMfor Test-Time Scaling in Tabular Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, under the Best-of-N evaluation, the second-stage RL policy optimization consistently improves performance over the SFT-only initialization.
Specifically, we observe that the average accuracy across all three tasks improves from 72.3% (SFT only) to 78.5% after RL training, yielding a total gain of 10.2%.
This demonstrates that bootstrapping from SFT provides a solid initialization, while RL optimization further enhances our PRM’s reasoning and tool-use effectiveness during the verification process.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>In-depth analysis of <span id="S5.T3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TaTToo</span>  on three table datasets. We evaluate the contributions of SFT and RL training stages, and assess the impact of reward shaping components during RL optimization.</figcaption>
